/-!
# `Rf_*`, `RΓ` and derived completion at an arbitrary finite-type sheaf of ideals

[Stacks, Tag 0A0G] asserts that `Rf_*` commutes with derived completion,
`Rf_*(K^∧) = (Rf_*K)^∧`, for a **finite type** sheaf of ideals `ℐ ⊂ 𝒪` and an **arbitrary**
`K ∈ D(𝒪')`; [Stacks, Tag 0A0I] is that display written in Section 52.7; [Stacks, Tag 0BLX] is
the special case `RΓ(𝒞, ℱ)^∧ = RΓ(𝒞, ℱ^∧)` together with the two spectral sequences it produces;
and [Stacks, Tag 0A0M] is the short exact sequence for `H^p(X, ℱ)^∧` which Tag 0BLX's identity
feeds. The first move of every one of those proofs is to invoke [Stacks, Tag 099F] — "by
Proposition 52.6.12 the derived completion functors exist" — which the source has already proved
from the finite-type hypothesis.

This library proves [Stacks, Tag 099F] for a sheaf of ideals *generated by finitely many global
sections* (`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete_generatedIdealSheaf`
of `GeneratedIdealAdjoint.lean`) and for one whose local sections are nilpotent
(`isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete_of_forall_isNilpotent` of `NilpotentIdeal.lean`), and the
four tags above are registered, honestly and partially, in those two regimes. At an arbitrary
finite-type sheaf of ideals Tag 099F rests on [Stacks, Tag 099E], the global object `K ⟶ 𝒪`
with `E^∧ = R𝓗om_𝒪(K, E)`; that object is now in this library, as `exists_isGlobalExtendedCech`
([Stacks, Tag 099E]) of `FiniteTypeExtendedCech.lean`, and the tag is carried on it by the
instance `isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete` ([Stacks, Tag 099F]) of `FiniteTypeReflector.lean`.

## Why nothing in this file carries a `@[stacks]` attribute

**This file registers no tag, and it is written that way on purpose.** Each statement below
once took the *conclusion* of [Stacks, Tag 099F] as a hypothesis — written as the instance binder

```
[(ObjectProperty.ι (AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.IsDerivedComplete X I')).IsRightAdjoint]
```

which is exactly what
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete_generatedIdealSheaf` supplies
for a *generated* ideal sheaf and what [Stacks, Tag 099E] supplies for an arbitrary finite-type
one: `exists_isGlobalExtendedCech` (`FiniteTypeExtendedCech.lean`, registered `@[stacks 099E]`)
has landed, and `FiniteTypeReflector.lean` registers the conclusion `@[stacks 099F]` as the
instance `isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete`, which this file imports — the binder therefore
resolves by instance search and no statement below still assumes it. A tag counts only on a
declaration proved under the source's own hypotheses, so
a statement that assumes a result the source *derives* before stating the tag must not carry the
tag; it is an engine. Consequently:

* no declaration in this file has a `@[stacks]` attribute: none should be given one while
  `hsource`, `htarget` and `hproj` — the conclusions of [Stacks, Tags 0A0E and 0944] which the
  source derives inside its own proofs — remain assumptions;
* the partial registrations the four tags already carry elsewhere — in
  `PushforwardCommutesCompletion.lean`, `PushforwardCommutesCompletionGenerated.lean`,
  `PushforwardCommutesCompletionSpaces.lean`, `GlobalSectionsCompletion.lean`,
  `GlobalSectionsCompletionGenerated.lean`, `UnboundedGlobalSections.lean` and
  `Completion/CohomologyAdicCompletion.lean` — are untouched by this file, which adds
  statements and removes nothing;
* with [Stacks, Tag 099E] available the instance binder is discharged — no proof here uses it
  for anything except to write `(-)^∧` down — and the remaining distance from each statement to
  a registration of its tag is exactly the discharge of `hsource`, `htarget` and `hproj`;

The adjoint was written as an instance binder while it was outstanding, so that it stayed
*visible* in the signature as an unfulfilled assumption; with `isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete`
now proved under `ℐ.toSheafOfModules.IsFiniteType` alone, instance search supplies it from the
finite-type datum — the way the source's Proposition 52.6.12 makes the functor available.

## What is carried besides [Stacks, Tag 099F], and why

Tag 099F is discharged by instance search (`isRightAdjoint_ι_isDerivedComplete`), but it was
**never** the only thing the full statements need: the extra inputs are carried explicitly and
named rather than hidden. The source's proof of Tag 0A0G, after invoking
Tag 099F, rewrites both sides by the Koszul formula `K^∧ = R lim (K ⊗^𝐋 K_n)` of
[Stacks, Tag 0A0E], exchanges `Rf_*` with `R lim`, and finishes with the projection formula
[Stacks, Tag 0944] together with `Lf^*K(𝒪, f_1^n, …, f_r^n) = K(𝒪', g_1^n, …, g_r^n)`. Of those
three steps **the exchange is available in this library at full generality** and the other two
are not, so the statements below carry the other two and prove the exchange:

* *the exchange with `R lim` is discharged.*
  `Stacks.Cohomology.DerivedPushforwardOnRingedTopoi.isDerivedLimit_map_derivedPushforward`
  ([Stacks, Tag 0A07]) applies verbatim to `Rf_*` along a morphism of ringed *sites*, and
  `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isDerivedLimit_map_derivedGlobalSectionsOverUnbounded`
  ([Stacks, Tag 08U1]) is its form for `RΓ(𝒞, -)`. Notes elsewhere in this directory calling
  this step unavailable are stale.
* *[Stacks, Tag 0A0E] is carried*, as the two hypotheses `hsource` and `htarget`: that the
  completion of the object in question is a derived limit of *some* `ℕᵒᵖ`-tower, on the source
  side and on the target side. The towers are arbitrary, which is what makes the statement
  usable: at a generated ideal sheaf `hsource` and `htarget` are
  `RingedSite.isDerivedLimit_tensorDerivedDualSystem_koszulPowerSystem_completion`
  of `KoszulPerfectDual.lean` at the Koszul tower, and on the `D(A)` side `htarget` is
  [Stacks, Tag 0EET], `AdicCompletionTor.derivedCompletion_homology_shortExact`, over a
  Noetherian ring.
* *[Stacks, Tag 0944] is carried*, as the hypothesis `hproj`: an isomorphism of towers
  `T' ⋙ Rf_* ≅ T`, which is the projection formula levelwise together with the identification of
  the pulled-back Koszul complexes. The library has only the projection formula's *map*,
  `Stacks.Cohomology.ProjectionFormulaOnRingedTopoi.projectionFormulaMap` of
  [Stacks, Tag 0B56], and not its invertibility.

Given those, the identification of the two sides is the uniqueness of derived limits,
`Rlim.Pro.nonempty_iso_of_isDerivedLimit_of_natIso` below, which reads [Stacks, Tag 0H9L] at a
plain isomorphism of towers. That is the mathematical content of each statement here.

## Main results

* `Rlim.Pro.nonempty_iso_of_isDerivedLimit_of_natIso`: derived limits of isomorphic
  `ℕᵒᵖ`-towers in a pretriangulated category are isomorphic.
* `RingedSite.nonempty_iso_derivedPushforward_derivedCompletion_of_isDerivedLimit`:
  **[Stacks, Tags 0A0G and 0A0I] for an arbitrary `K` at an arbitrary finite-type pair of ideal
  sheaves**, `Rf_*(K^∧) ≅ (Rf_*K)^∧`, with the adjoint of [Stacks, Tag 099F] discharged by
  instance search and [Stacks, Tags 0A0E and 0944] carried as `hsource`, `htarget`, `hproj`.
* `RingedSite.exists_iso_derivedCompletion_derivedGlobalSections_of_isDerivedLimit`:
  **[Stacks, Tag 0BLX]'s identity `RΓ(𝒞, ℱ)^∧ = RΓ(𝒞, ℱ^∧)` at an arbitrary finite-type ideal
  sheaf**, with the same three inputs; the derived completion functor on `D(A)` is produced
  rather than assumed, by [Stacks, Tag 0920].
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.exists_pages_derivedCompletion_globalSections_of_isGE`:
  **[Stacks, Tag 0BLX]'s second spectral sequence at an arbitrary finite-type ideal sheaf**, from
  the cohomological boundedness below of `ℱ^∧`, the adjoint being supplied by instance search.
* `Stacks.Algebraization.exists_shortExact_adicCompletion_cohomology_of_isDerivedLimit`:
  **[Stacks, Tag 0A0M]'s short exact sequence with `H^p(X, ℱ)^∧` in the middle**, under the
  source's own hypotheses and one named input, the levelwise comparison
  `RΓ(X, ℱ/𝓘ⁿℱ) ≅ RΓ(X, ℱ) ⊗^𝐋_A A/Iⁿ`. **This one needs no part of [Stacks, Tag 099F]** — see
  below.

## Statement by statement

### Tags 0A0G and 0A0I, for an arbitrary object

`nonempty_iso_derivedPushforward_derivedCompletion_of_isDerivedLimit` is the display
`Rf_*(K^∧) = (Rf_*K)^∧` for an arbitrary `K ∈ D(𝒪')`, which is the whole assertion of
[Stacks, Tag 0A0G] and the display of [Stacks, Tag 0A0I]. It is stated at an arbitrary pair of
finite-type ideal sheaves, `f^♯` carrying sections of `ℐ` into `ℐ'`
(`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.PushforwardIdealCompatible`, the source's own relation between
the two ideals), with the hypotheses listed in "What is carried" above.

It is the display as the source writes it — an isomorphism of the two objects — and not the
stronger assertion that the *canonical* comparison map
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison` is invertible. Uniqueness
of derived limits is not canonical, so the isomorphism this produces is not known to be that
map; `isIso_derivedCompletionPushforwardComparison_of_isIso_map_unit` of
`PushforwardCommutesCompletion.lean` is the criterion that would be needed, and its hypothesis
is not what the derived-limit route delivers.

### Tag 0BLX's identity

`exists_iso_derivedCompletion_derivedGlobalSections_of_isDerivedLimit` is the same argument with
`RΓ(𝒞, -) : D(𝒪) ⥤ D(A)` in place of `Rf_*`, which is what the source's one-line proof — Tag
0A0G at the morphism of ringed topoi `(𝒞, 𝒪) ⟶ (pt, A)` — amounts to; there is no such morphism
in this library, so the argument is run again rather than specialized. The source's hypotheses
`I` finitely generated and `𝓘 = I𝒪`
(`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.IsIdealSheafGeneratedBy`) are kept, and the reflector on `D(A)` is
produced from the first of them by `AdicFilteredCompletion`, so no derived completion functor on
`D(A)` is assumed. `htarget` is quantified over reflectors because the library fixes no choice of
one; by uniqueness of left adjoints that is no stronger than naming one.

The statement registered in the nilpotent regime,
`exists_iso_derivedCompletion_derivedGlobalSections` of `UnboundedGlobalSections.lean`, is not
an instance of this one: there both completions are the identity and no derived limit is used.

### Tag 0BLX's second spectral sequence

`exists_pages_derivedCompletion_globalSections_of_isGE` is
`exists_pages_derivedCompletion_globalSections` of `GlobalSectionsCompletion.lean` with the
second of that theorem's two assumed inputs replaced by something weaker and named. That theorem
assumes a bounded below complex representing `ℱ^∧` with an isomorphism; this one assumes
only that `ℱ^∧` is cohomologically `≥ b` for some `b`, and *produces* the complex, the
isomorphism and the Cartan-Eilenberg resolution, by `DerivedCategory.exists_iso_Q_obj_of_isGE`
and [Stacks, Tag 015I]. Boundedness below is the one aspect of `exists_isGlobalExtendedCech`
([Stacks, Tag 099E]) that this statement needs beyond the adjoint: the object `K ⟶ 𝒪` of that
tag is locally a Čech complex of
some length `r`, so `R𝓗om_𝒪(K, ℱ)` is cohomologically bounded below — which is precisely how
`isGE_derivedCompletion_generatedIdealSheaf_obj` of `GlobalSectionsCompletionGenerated.lean`
discharges it in the globally generated case.

### Tag 0A0M

`exists_shortExact_adicCompletion_cohomology_of_isDerivedLimit` is the transport of
`Stacks.Algebraization.exists_shortExact_cohomology_limit_adicQuotients` along [Stacks, Tag
0BLX], which `exists_shortExact_adicCompletion_cohomology` of
`Completion/CohomologyAdicCompletion.lean` performs with that identification as the hypothesis
`hBLX`. Here `hBLX` is **proved**, from one named input, and so does not appear:

* the formal completion side is a derived limit of the adic quotient tower's derived global
  sections, by [Stacks, Tag 0A0K]
  (`Stacks.Algebraization.isDerivedLimit_single_limit_powerQuotients`) followed by
  [Stacks, Tag 08U1] (`AlgebraicGeometry.isDerivedLimit_map_derivedGlobalSectionsOver`);
* the derived `I`-adic completion of `RΓ(X, ℱ)` is a derived limit of its ideal-power tower,
  `A` being Noetherian, by [Stacks, Tag 0EET]
  (`AdicCompletionTor.derivedCompletion_homology_shortExact`);
* `hproj` identifies the two towers: `RΓ(X, ℱ/𝓘ⁿℱ) ≅ RΓ(X, ℱ) ⊗^𝐋_A A/Iⁿ`, levelwise and
  compatibly with the transition maps. This is the geometric half of [Stacks, Tag 0944] that the
  library has only for a principal ideal
  (`Stacks.Cohomology.KoszulTowerSections.nonempty_iso_koszulTower_derivedSections`,
  [Stacks, Tag 0H3C]).

So this statement carries **no** part of [Stacks, Tags 099E or 099F]: the reflector comes from
[Stacks, Tag 0920], and the left hand side of `hBLX` is the formal completion `lim ℱ/𝓘ⁿℱ` rather
than a reflector applied to anything. Its single assumed input is the levelwise projection
formula. The properness clause of the tag — the vanishing of the `R¹lim` term — is not here: it
rests on [Stacks, Tag 02OB], which this library does not have, although the proper mapping
theorem [Stacks, Tag 02O5] below that is available.

This statement lives here rather than beside its engine because
`Completion/CohomologyAdicCompletion.lean` follows a different route to the same tag —
[Stacks, Tag 0BLD] at a *principal* ideal.

## Implementation notes

*The derived category instances.* `HasDerivedCategory.standard` is given a high local priority in
the ringed-site namespace, and `Stacks.Algebraization.modulesHasDerivedCategory` in the scheme
section, each as the file it is borrowed from does. Without the priorities the two halves of the
file disagree about which model of `D(SheafOfModules …)` they mean and the universe levels of
`Rf_*` and of an `ℕᵒᵖ`-tower no longer match.

*The `HasProduct` arguments.* `CategoryTheory.Pretriangulated.IsDerivedLimit` can only be written
at a tower whose countable product exists, so each tower carries that instance; the exchange
lemmas return the product of the image tower inside their existential, and it is re-`have`d at
the spelling instance search needs because `Opposite.op n` and the anonymous constructor `⟨n⟩`
are definitionally but not reducibly equal.

*What would strengthen these statements.* At a sheaf of ideals generated by finitely many global
sections `hsource` and `htarget` can be discharged outright — Tag 0A0E is proved there and the
reflector is the Čech completion — leaving `hproj` as the only hypothesis, i.e. Tag 0A0G for an
arbitrary `K` modulo the projection formula alone. That statement would still be an engine, Tag
0944 being a result the source's proof invokes, so it is recorded here as the next step rather
than written.

